      <p>If your clinic is not listed, the same approach applies. Dental clinics and med spas have their own pages because their treatments and rules need more detail.</p>

      <h2>Separate urgent, routine and high-value care</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Urgent:</strong> emergency dental, urgent vet visits, acute injuries. Call-led, and only worth running when someone can answer and offer an appointment</li>
        <li><strong>Routine:</strong> check-ups, vaccinations, hygiene, follow-up care. Steady demand and the start of a long relationship with the patient or pet owner</li>
        <li><strong>High-value treatment:</strong> implants, aligners, cosmetic and aesthetic procedures, fertility treatment. Long decisions, high value, and campaigns of their own</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When everything shares one budget, cheap routine clicks use the money that high-value treatment needs.</p>

      <h2>Work within health advertising rules</h2>
      <p>Human healthcare ads follow Google's healthcare and medicines policies. Ads should not promise outcomes, treatments must be described accurately, and some services need certification before ads run in some countries, such as addiction treatment. Google also restricts personalised advertising around health, so remarketing and some audience types may not be allowed. Veterinary clinics face fewer of these limits, though claims still need to be accurate.</p>
      <p>Privacy matters too. Pages where patients book or share health details need careful handling of tracking, and some countries have specific health privacy laws. Collect only what you need in forms, and never ask for medical details in an ad landing page form.</p>

      <h2>Be available when people search</h2>
      <p>Urgent searches for a dentist, doctor or vet often come in the evening and at weekends. If the clinic cannot see anyone then, ads should not run then, or should clearly offer the next available appointment. Missed calls from ads are paid for and usually lost to the next clinic.</p>

      <h2>Measure appointments, not enquiries</h2>
      <p>A call is not a booking. Track booked appointments and, for high-value treatment, accepted treatment plans, recorded from the practice system. <a href="/learn/dental-google-ads-conversion-tracking/">How to track new patients from dental Google Ads</a> shows the setup, and the same method works for any clinic.</p>

      <h2>Where clinic budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Job and training searches for nurses, hygienists, vets and therapists</li>
        <li>Home remedy and symptom searches with no intent to book</li>
        <li>Free or public care you do not provide</li>
        <li>Product searches, such as supplements, pet food or at-home kits</li>
        <li>Areas further away than patients or pet owners will travel</li>
      </ul>

